 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to approved costs of providing special education services to children with handicapping conditions in preschool",
    "basePrintNo": "S6273",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6273-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §4410, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6273\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\napproved costs of providing special education services to children\nwith handicapping conditions in preschool\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis legislation clarifies that facility-related costs of certain\nfacilities and non-direct care service costs associated with\nidentified space directly related to special education itinerant\nservices for children diagnosed with autism spectrum disorder are\nreimbursable\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one amends Education Law § 4410 (10) to add a new paragraph\n(f) to provide that reimbursement shall be made for the costs of\ncertain property and non-direct care service associated with\nidentified space directly related to special education itinerant\nservices (SEIT services) and used to ensure that the child receiving\nsuch services derives the fullest educational benefit. Such costs\nshall include those associated with discrete, specific space located\noutside of the classroom but within the proximity of such classroom\nwhere the special education itinerant services are provided. Such\ncosts shall be calculated and reimbursed using an allocation\nmethodology as determined by the Commissioner's representatives and\nshall include mortgage interest, depreciation, storage, housekeeping\nand supplies, and non-direct care personal services costs associated\nwith maintenance employees.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nSpecial Education Itinerant Teacher (SEIT) services for children\ndiagnosed with Autism Spectrum Disorder are more than just \"pull-out\"\nor \"push-in\" services, but rather are part of a unique program which\nallows autistic children to be fully integrated into classrooms with\ntypically developing students. The children being serviced at these\nlocations have multiple, complex challenges such as self-stimulatory\nbehaviors, lack of cooperation, obsessions, rigidity, shut down,\nstimuli overload and distraction. In order to address their\nchallenges, and in order to implement their Individualized Education\nPrograms (IEPs), skills must be introduced, taught and reinforced both\nin a natural setting, the classroom, and in a contrived setting,\noutside of the classroom.\n\nIn the SEIT service space, the presence and position of furniture and\nsupplies are precisely monitored to eliminate distractions. These\nspaces are a natural consequence of individualized SEIT services and\nare undeniably an essential program expense. Facilities-related costs\nfor these spaces are simply a fair allocation of the SEIT services to\nthe full program.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nMinimal. Any costs would be offset by the reduced need for\nintervention going forward.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
